{"kind":"request","text":"claude: close the rest of the departed-author false-offer family in the decision-records UI. This is the durable child codex's verdict 59c1d5c4 requires to exist before that head can be approved, and filing it is mine.\n\nWHERE THIS CAME FROM, AND CREDIT WHERE IT IS DUE. You found this yourself while repairing something else, disclosed it in artifact a38ff936, and correctly refused to implement it unauthorized — you wrote that it wants its own child request and that you had not written yourself one. That was the right call. Codex then made it a condition of approval in 59c1d5c4: the requested false ratification offer is closed and fail-closed, but approval is blocked until this shape has a durable owner. So the row exists now, and it is yours.\n\nTHE DEFECT. fold.go refuses any record whose author is not a live participant. semanticActions does not ask that question: it still offers a departed viewer 'needs work' and 'withdraw' on their own rows. It is the same fail-open you closed in mayRatify — reading presence in the roster as membership, when the fold deliberately keeps a departed principal there with retired=true and no roles precisely because their signatures are permanent. Different sites, identical mistake. Nothing unauthorized can land, because the server still refuses; the harm is that the screen invites a signature it knows will be rejected, and each attempt writes a permanent ineffective row.\n\nWHY IT IS SEQUENCED RATHER THAN STARTED NOW. The sites you would edit live at paths 6e29fa99 is about to rewrite, and its five artifacts are the predecessors that merge will retire. Starting before the merge would mean cutting from a base that is about to move and publishing artifacts against predecessors. This request deliberately rests on my implementation request d14e96fb and codex's verdict 59c1d5c4, not on any artifact, so the merge cannot stale it. That was codex's suggestion and it is better than my earlier plan of holding the filing until after the merge: this way the defect has an owner before approval, which is what the verdict actually asked for.\n\nI HAVE NOT VERIFIED THE DEFECT MYSELF. I am filing on your disclosure and codex's independent reading of it, both of which describe the same sites. If you find on inspection that a site named here does not behave as described, say so on this row rather than implementing around it.\n\nConditions are in body.conditions, and condition (1) is deliberately not limited to the two actions you named: check the whole file for the same shape.","body":{"conditions":"SEQUENCING FIRST. Do not implement or edit any head under this request until commit 6e29fa9935c262f8b3a2eb232a8fe80324f346ff is merged to main. Until then this row is an owned, open assignment and nothing more. Promise it whenever you like; the promise records the owner, not a licence to start.\n\nTHEN, on a new branch cut from the post-merge main: make every action the decision-records UI offers agree with what the fold will admit, for the departed-author case. (1) semanticActions no longer offers a departed viewer any action on their own rows that internal/workroom/fold.go would refuse for want of live participation — at minimum 'needs work' and 'withdraw', and any other action in the same file that turns on authorship rather than on a live role. (2) The membership test is the same fold-equivalent predicate the merged repair already uses for mayRatify: the projected participant role, not entry-existence in Projection.Actors. If that predicate is duplicated at more than one site, factor it to one place and use it everywhere. (3) A rendered or semantic regression covers each newly-guarded action for the departed-author shape (retired=true, no roles, viewer fingerprint equal to the record's author), and each regression fails against the unrepaired code. Demonstrate that by mutation and say so in the artifact: reinstate the old check, name the assertion that fires, and confirm the file hashes identical afterwards. (4) Say explicitly in the artifact whether any further false-offer site remains in the UI after this head, so the next reviewer does not have to rediscover it. If one remains, name it and leave it; do not widen scope. (5) Fresh artifacts at every maintained path the head changes, and an independent review requested from an actor who is neither you nor the author of any artifact at that head. (6) The gates pass at the exact head with -count=1: gofmt -l empty, go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test -count=1 ./..., npm test --prefix ui, make ui-check with internal/service/uidist byte-reproduced, and git diff --check.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}